🚀 Week 4 – Day 6 Garbage Collection & finalize() Method in Java ☕

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧠 Introduction

Java automatically manages memory using **Garbage Collection (GC)**.

👉 Garbage Collection helps in:

✔ Removing unused objects
✔ Freeing heap memory
✔ Improving memory efficiency

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

♻️ 1. What is Garbage Collection?

Garbage Collection is the process of automatically destroying unused objects.

---

📌 Example

```java id="g1"
Student s = new Student();
s = null;
```

👉 The object becomes eligible for Garbage Collection.

---

🔍 Why Garbage Collection?

✔ Prevents memory leaks
✔ Reduces manual memory management
✔ Improves application stability

---

🏗️ 2. Objects Stored in Heap

All objects are created inside heap memory.

```java id="g2"
Book b = new Book();
```

---

📌 Eligible for Garbage Collection

An object becomes eligible when it is no longer reachable.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 Ways to Make Objects Eligible

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1️⃣ Nullifying Reference

```java id="g3"
Student s = new Student();
s = null;
```

---

2️⃣ Reassigning Reference

```java id="g4"
Student s1 = new Student();
Student s2 = new Student();

s1 = s2;
```

👉 First object becomes unreachable.

---

3️⃣ Anonymous Objects

```java id="g5"
new Student();
```

---

4️⃣ Objects Inside Method

```java id="g6"
void test() {
    Student s = new Student();
}
```

👉 Object becomes unreachable after method execution.

---

⚡ 3. Requesting Garbage Collection

We can request JVM to run GC using:

```java id="g7"
System.gc();
```

OR

```java id="g8"
Runtime.getRuntime().gc();
```

⚠️ JVM may ignore the request.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🛠️ 4. finalize() Method

`finalize()` is called before object destruction by GC.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Syntax

```java id="f1"
protected void finalize() {
    System.out.println("Object destroyed");
}
```

---

💻 Example

```java id="f2"
class Demo {

    protected void finalize() {
        System.out.println("Finalize method called");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Demo d = new Demo();

        d = null;

        System.gc();
    }
}
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

⚠️ Important Note

`finalize()` is deprecated in modern Java versions.

👉 Not recommended for resource cleanup.

---

🔍 Why finalize() is Deprecated?

❌ Unpredictable execution
❌ Performance overhead
❌ Security issues

---

✅ Modern Alternative

Use:

✔ try-with-resources
✔ Explicit resource closing

---

# 📊 Garbage Collection Process

```text id="g9"
Create Object → Use Object → Object Becomes Unreachable → GC Removes Object
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🎯 Advantages of Garbage Collection

✔ Automatic memory management
✔ Prevents dangling pointers
✔ Reduces programmer effort

---

⚠️ Common Issues

❌ Excessive object creation
❌ Memory leaks due to references
❌ Assuming GC runs immediately

---

🧠 Real Insight

👉 Garbage Collection is one reason Java is safer than C/C++ because developers do not manually free memory.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📚 Key Takeaways

✔ Garbage Collection removes unused objects
✔ Objects in heap can become unreachable
✔ `System.gc()` requests GC execution
✔ `finalize()` is deprecated
✔ Modern Java prefers automatic resource management

